{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusader states a military leader and a shrewd politician. His reign was marked by both military campaigns and diplomatic maneuvering aimed at securing and expanding the county's territory and influence.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond was born around 1120 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and his wife Countess Sophia of Calabria. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1154. His early years were spent consolidating his authority dealing with internal dissent and maintaining relations with neighboring powers. He faced challenges from Muslim forces and rival Crusader lords requiring him to demonstrate military prowess and political skill. His father's death presented an opportunity for Raymond to solidify his position as the ruler of Tripoli a strategically important county in the Levant.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw significant military activity. He engaged in numerous campaigns against the Ayyubid dynasty which controlled much of Syria and Palestine. These campaigns often involved sieges battles and skirmishes aimed at disrupting Ayyubid control and protecting Tripoli's borders. He also dealt with local Muslim rulers seeking to maintain a fragile peace and secure trade routes. Raymond actively sought alliances with other Crusader states particularly the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Principality of Antioch strengthening the Crusader presence in the region.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond III was known for his diplomatic skills. He understood the importance of alliances in maintaining power and security in a volatile region. He forged alliances with various Crusader leaders and even with some Muslim rulers when it served his interests. He actively engaged in negotiations seeking to secure favorable terms for Tripoli and its inhabitants. His diplomatic efforts were crucial in navigating the complex political landscape of the Crusader states and maintaining a balance of power. He fostered relationships with European powers seeking support and resources for his campaigns.\n\n*Later Years and Legacy*\n\nIn his later years Raymond III continued to face challenges from the Ayyubids. He maintained a strong military force and continued to engage in defensive campaigns. He also focused on strengthening the economy of Tripoli promoting trade and attracting settlers. Raymond III died in 1199 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV. His reign is remembered as a period of stability and prosperity for the County of Tripoli. He is regarded as a capable ruler a skilled military leader and a shrewd diplomat who played a vital role in the history of the Crusader states. His actions helped to preserve Tripoli's independence and maintain a degree of autonomy in a challenging environment. He left a legacy of strength resilience and political acumen.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.20648594200611115}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.054002758115530014}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00023135847004596144}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.5987286790041253e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.583032467868179e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -2.2649508537142538e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.0034172493033111095}, {"token": "▁until", "logprob": -0.03166874870657921}, {"token": "▁his", "logprob": -0.00028081765049137175}, {"token": "▁death", "logprob": -1.4305012882